+# Decision: Change-delivery approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard commits every edit as one authoritative event revision and must get those revisions in front of up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers per event. Committing an edit and showing it are separable problems: commands arrive over HTTPS regardless, but the route from a committed revision to a browser is a free choice, and it is the choice that sizes three of the four design nodes. The change store's read indexes and publication machinery, delivery's entire responsibility, and the browser's subscription and recovery behavior all follow from it; picking wrong means rebuilding all three rather than swapping a transport.
+
+The board's required freshness is modest — 15 seconds under normal connectivity — and every candidate route clears it, so this decision is not about meeting a requirement. It is about what MarketBoard pays, in money and in operations, for updates faster than it strictly needs, and about who owns the delivery path when it breaks during published event hours.
+
+No binary gate separates the candidates. All three can meet the 15-second freshness goal, 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, recovery after a 20-minute disconnect, and 99.5 percent availability during event hours; all three preserve one authoritative revision per committed edit; and the project's tenets explicitly permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. Nothing is eliminated outright, so the decision turns entirely on graded criteria.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Recurring external cost — major.** What the delivery route costs per month to run, excluding build effort. MarketBoard is a pilot across forty markets with no revenue of its own; a per-connection or per-message bill scales with exactly the thing the project is built to do.
+
+**Control of the delivery path — major.** Whether the project can change delivery behavior — fan-out rules, backpressure, catch-up semantics, retention of undelivered revisions — without a provider's agreement, and whether an outage in the delivery path is one the project can diagnose and fix. The tenets permit managed infrastructure, so this criterion is a project preference rather than a tenet obligation: it carries weight but cannot eliminate an option on principle.
+
+**Connection and reconnect operations owned — major.** The standing operational load the route creates: sizing and watching connection capacity, surviving a socket tier restart mid-event, and handling the mass-reconnect that follows one. This is load that recurs every event, not one-time build effort.
+
+**Update latency beyond the 15-second floor — moderate.** How fresh the board actually is in practice. Subsecond updates are useful to coordinators reacting to a stall closure at opening time, but they are not required, which caps this criterion below the cost and operations criteria.
+
+**Failure visibility in the browser — moderate.** How readily the route lets a browser tell "nothing has changed" apart from "I am no longer hearing about changes." This criterion derives from the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*; the tenet is satisfiable on any of these routes, so what varies is how much mechanism each one needs to satisfy it.
+
+**Browser-side recovery complexity — moderate.** How much subscription, disconnect-detection, and catch-up logic lives in browser code, where it is hardest to observe when it misbehaves and runs across whatever browsers coordinators' staff actually use.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board on a five-second timer, sending the revision they last saw; the server answers with the revisions committed since that cursor, or an empty response. There are no long-lived connections and no server-side session state — every request is independently authorized and independently answerable, so any web server behind any load balancer can serve it, and a server restart is invisible to browsers. The change store needs cursor queries over revision history; delivery stays ordinary request/response; browsers run a timer.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Recurring external cost: **strong** fit. No vendor; the cost is the read load of 800 browsers polling every five seconds against an event that changes about twice a minute.
+- Control of the delivery path: **strong** fit. The entire route is project code on project infrastructure.
+- Connection and reconnect operations owned: **strong** fit. There is no connection state to size or restore; a restart needs no mass-reconnect handling.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **weak** fit. Every update waits for the next tick, so a change lands in browsers up to five seconds after it commits plus request time.
+- Failure visibility in the browser: **strong** fit. A poll either returns or fails, and consecutive failures are directly observable as loss of contact.
+- Browser-side recovery complexity: **strong** fit. Recovery is the normal path — the next successful poll carries whatever was missed, however long the gap.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest and simplest route to operate, paid for with a few seconds of lag on every update and continuous reads against a mostly unchanged board.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** Browsers hold an authenticated WebSocket connection and subscribe to an event; when a command commits, the server publishes the new revision and it is fanned out to that event's subscribed sessions immediately. Behind it, the change store makes revisions replayable by revision number and publishes committed revisions reliably enough that a live subscriber is not silently skipped; a browser that reconnects names the last revision it holds and replays forward. The project runs the socket tier, so fan-out rules, backpressure, and catch-up semantics are all its own code.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Recurring external cost: **strong** fit. No vendor fees; the cost is compute holding idle connections.
+- Control of the delivery path: **strong** fit. Every part of the route is project code, debuggable and changeable without a provider.
+- Connection and reconnect operations owned: **weak** fit. Connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers is the project's to size and watch, and any socket tier restart produces a simultaneous reconnect-and-replay from every connected browser.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **strong** fit. Revisions are pushed as they commit, with no polling interval in the path.
+- Failure visibility in the browser: **partial** fit. A silent board and a dead connection look identical over an idle socket, so visibility depends on liveness mechanism the project builds and maintains rather than on the transport.
+- Browser-side recovery complexity: **weak** fit. Browsers carry subscription state, disconnect detection, reconnect with backoff, and replay from a held revision — the logic the other two routes either avoid or inherit.
+
+**Summary.** Low-latency updates with nothing bought in, paid for with connection capacity and reconnect behavior the project owns in perpetuity.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A realtime provider holds the browser connections and operates the fan-out. The server publishes committed revisions into the provider's channels and reconciles what it published against what the provider accepted; browsers use the provider's client SDK, which supplies connection state, reconnection, and whatever catch-up window the provider offers. Connection capacity, scaling, and reconnect storms are the provider's operational problem, and its client libraries arrive already hardened against real-world browsers and networks.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Recurring external cost: **weak** fit. Fees recur for the life of the project and scale with connections and messages, which is the load MarketBoard exists to generate.
+- Control of the delivery path: **weak** fit. Fan-out semantics, catch-up window, and outage response are the provider's; changing delivery behavior means working within its subscription model, and provider credentials become part of the delivery path.
+- Connection and reconnect operations owned: **strong** fit. Connection capacity and reconnect handling are bought rather than operated.
+- Update latency beyond the floor: **strong** fit. Push delivery with no polling interval, on infrastructure built for it.
+- Failure visibility in the browser: **strong** fit. Connection state is a first-class part of the provider's client model, so distinguishing a quiet board from a lost connection is a supported query rather than project mechanism.
+- Browser-side recovery complexity: **partial** fit. Reconnection and buffering come from the SDK, but reconciling the provider's catch-up window against the authoritative revision history remains project logic.
+
+**Summary.** The fastest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations, paid for in recurring fees and a delivery path the project does not control.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+Two of the three major criteria point away from the hosted service and toward a route the project runs itself: recurring fees scale with the project's own success, and a delivery path the project cannot change or debug is a poor foundation for the three nodes being sized around it. That leaves polling and self-hosted sockets, which are indistinguishable on cost and control, and differ on the remaining major criterion — operations — in the opposite direction from latency. Polling wins operations outright: no connection capacity, no reconnect storms, and recovery that is simply the normal path. Self-hosted sockets win latency, and win it well past the 15-second requirement.
+
+The choice is therefore a deliberate trade of a major criterion for a moderate one: MarketBoard accepts the connection and reconnect operations, plus the browser-side recovery logic, to get updates that land as they commit. The justification is not in the criteria weights but in the domain behind them — coordinators are reacting to stall closures and power failures during an opening-time burst, and a board that is five seconds stale on every change is a board people stop trusting mid-burst. The 15-second goal is a floor for acceptability, not a description of what the product should feel like.
+
+This decision is taken with the tenet *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*, which declines to settle the question and leaves it to criteria, and against the grain of no tenet. It does put the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* under more strain than the alternatives would: an idle socket is indistinguishable from a quiet board without liveness mechanism, so satisfying that tenet becomes project work rather than a property of the transport.
+
+Committing forecloses a stateless delivery tier: delivery now holds per-session state, which constrains how it is deployed, restarted, and eventually scaled beyond one process. It obligates three things downstream — the change store must make revisions replayable by revision number and publish committed revisions without silently skipping a live subscriber; delivery must own socket session lifecycle, per-event subscriptions, and fan-out; and browser sync must own disconnect detection, reconnect, and catch-up replay from the last held revision, including the liveness signal that keeps a dead connection from reading as a quiet board.
