 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over WebSocket connections this project hosts itself: the server publishes each newly committed event revision to the sessions subscribed to that event, and a browser that has fallen behind catches up by replaying revisions from the last one it holds.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around that route. The change store keeps event revisions replayable by revision number and publishes committed revisions reliably enough that a live subscriber is not silently left behind. Delivery owns socket sessions: accepting subscriptions, tracking which sessions follow which event, and fanning committed revisions out to them. Browsers own disconnect detection, reconnection, and requesting the revisions they missed while away.
+
+Hosting the socket tier means this project owns connection capacity and reconnect behavior rather than buying them, and runs no realtime vendor in the path of a committed edit.
 
